WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.300
Follow LangFocus on Facebook, Twitter and Instagram.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:07.940
Hello everyone. Welcome to the LangFocus Channel

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:08.780
and my name is Paul

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:12.560
Today's video is Mystery Languages 4

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:14.620
Can you guess the mystery languages?

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:18.260
For each language I'll give you an audio sample to listen to,

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:22.520
and after that I'll give you a written sample of the language, so you can see what it looks like.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:26.580
And then I'll give a moment to make a note of your answer, to make a note of you've guess,

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.700
before I reveal the answer to you.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.920
So let's begin with the first language.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.060
That one was actually a giraffe.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:47.720
Giraffes normally communicate through infrasonic sounds that humans can't hear.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:51.420
So you problably won't hear a giraffe making sounds like that anytime soon.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.100
So I guess we'll just move on to a human language.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:54.800
Please listen.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:15.380
Now some written examples

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.480
So what language is it?

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:25.960
.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:27.820
..

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:30.600
...

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:32.840
The language is Tok Pisin

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:37.560
Tok Pisin is English-based creole that is spoken in
Papua New Guinea.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.480
You may have noticed some English words,

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:42.340
or some words that sounded  strangely similar to English

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:44.020
but pronounced differently.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.600
That's because most of Tok Pisin's vocabulary came from English

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.280
while the basis if its grammar came from local languages.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.620
Let's take a look at some of those sentences again.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.960
"Buk bilong husat" means "Whose book?" or "Whose book is this?"

00:01:58.660 --> 00:01:59.460
"Buk" means "book".

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:04.200
"Bilong" comes from "belong" but is used to show possession in Tok Pisin.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.000
"Husat" means "who",

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:08.540
and I think it comes from "who's that".

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:10.100
So word for word it's...

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:11.800
Book-belong-who?

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.120
"Em i gat planti moni" means "He has much money".

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.460
"Em" means he,  it comes from "him", actually.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.200
"I" is a word that comes before of predicate.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.800
"Gat" means "has", and it comes from English "got".

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.480
"Planti" means "much" or "plenty".

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:30.960
And "moni" means "money", of course.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.480
"Mi no kam bikos mi sik" means: "I didn't come because I'm sick".

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:39.480
The verb tense is not really specified but it would be clear from the context.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:40.900
"Mi" means "I"

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.160
and It's comes English "me",

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:47.300
because in Tok Pisin there's no distinction between the subject and the object pronoun.

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:48.940
"No" shows a negative;

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:50.420
"kam" means "come";

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:51.720
"bikos" means "because";

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:53.380
then there's "mi" again;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.040
and "sik" means "sick".

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.240
So you can see that speaking Tok Pisin is it's sort of like speaking English

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:02.680
with a kind of simplified grammar and some different pronunciation .

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.440
But of course there is more to it than that.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.120
Ok, on to the next language

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.100
Now let's see some written samples.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.340
So what language do you think it is?

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:39.960
The language is Zulu.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:43.360
Zulu is a member of the Bantu language family

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.560
and is spoken by around 10 million people

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:49.060
mainly in
South Africa, but also in nearby Botswana, Lesotho,

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:52.180
Malawi, Mozambique and Swaziland.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:55.860
One feature you may have noticed while listening was some “click” sounds.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.520
Listen to this phrase again:

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.960
Notice that the letter “c” stands for a click sound here.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:07.620
And here's a short clip from that audio sample again.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:09.180
This time, listen for the clicks.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:21.220
Clicks are a linguistic feature found almost
exclusively in African languages,

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:24.180
specifically in the Bantu and Cushitic language families.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.080
Ok, on to the next language.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:53.360
And now let's *see* the language.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:03.280
So, what's the mystery language?

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:08.440
It's Polish.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.200
Polish is a member of the Slavic language family

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:15.080
so if you guessed a different Slavic language like
Russian, then you were pretty close.

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:18.960
Polish has around 55 million native speakers, mainly in Poland,

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:22.040
but it's also spoken by Polish minorities in other countries.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:24.520
Here are some hints that this language is Polish.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:27.940
There are numerous diacritic marks, including the
“kreska”

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:29.820
(which looks like an acute accent),

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:33.180
and the ogonek, like a little tail attached to these vowels.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:37.920
there are also digraphs and trigraphs that represent single consonant sounds,

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:42.180
like these ones. So you'll see these combinations of letters quite frequently in Polish.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:44.680
You'll also see the letter “y”
used as a vowel,

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:49.340
so it often appears between consonants or after a consonant at the end of a word.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:52.420
If you'd like to learn more about the Slavic language family,

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:55.140
check out my video on the subject right here.

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:57.680
I hope you had fun guessing today's mystery languages.

